---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Brief = `No brief this time. Build something that uses a loop and a
decision. What it does is up to you. It has to be something a
person could actually run and get something out of.`;

const SampleOutput = `(There is no sample output. It is your program.)`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/cc-set-3" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back to Set 3
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge: Open Brief — Your Own Program</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Brief</h2>

          <Code
            code={Brief}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="What they asked for"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Write your requirements list before you write any code.</b> Input,
            calculate, output, and anything you had to decide for yourself. See{" "}
            <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/cc-set-3">Set 3</a> for what that looks like.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="notes">
          <h2>Notes</h2>

          <ul>
            <li><b>Write your plan first and submit it with the code.</b> Not a requirements list this time &mdash; a plan, in plain sentences, describing what the program does step by step.</li>
            <li>Keep it small. A person should be able to use it in under two minutes.</li>
            <li>It must have at least one loop and at least one conditional. Everything else is your call.</li>
            <li>If you cannot think of anything: a menu that repeats, a quiz with a score, a savings calculator, a dice game that plays until someone wins.</li>
            <li>
              If your program is the kind a person would want to use more than
              once, <code>confirm("Go again?")</code> is the shortest way to let
              them. It satisfies the loop requirement on its own, though a program
              whose <i>only</i> loop is the go-again wrapper is a thin one &mdash;
              aim for a loop that does real work too.
            </li>
            <li><b>This is the only page in the quarter with a blank page on it.</b> That is deliberate &mdash; Quarter 2 opens with a two-session build where you choose your own project, and this is the small version of that.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>Sample Output</h2>

          <Code
            code={SampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            title="Sample Output"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-3">Your plan is graded on whether it exists and matches what you built, exactly like a requirements list.</p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your requirements list and your program file, named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">cc-open-loop</b>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
